---
description: Extract repeated or domain-meaningful string literals into named constants or const arrays
globs: "**/*.{ts,tsx}"
alwaysApply: false
---

# String literals: extract into typed constants

Never scatter raw string literals that represent a fixed set of values (modes, statuses, roles, categories, etc.) across multiple files. Define the type in `lib/types.ts` and the named constants in `lib/constants.ts`.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — duplicated string literals, no single source of truth
const [mode, setMode] = useState<'edit' | 'preview'>('edit');
if (mode === 'edit') { /* … */ }

// ✅ GOOD — type in types.ts, constants in constants.ts
// lib/types.ts
export type PreviewMode = 'edit' | 'preview';

// lib/constants.ts
import type { PreviewMode } from './types';
export const PREVIEW_MODES: readonly PreviewMode[] = ['edit', 'preview'];
export const PREVIEW_EDIT: PreviewMode = 'edit';
export const PREVIEW_PREVIEW: PreviewMode = 'preview';

// consumer.tsx
import { PREVIEW_EDIT } from '@/lib/constants';
import type { PreviewMode } from '@/lib/types';
const [mode, setMode] = useState<PreviewMode>(PREVIEW_EDIT);
```

**When to apply:**
- Two or more files reference the same string values
- A value belongs to a closed set (enum-like) rather than free-form user input
- The string appears in type annotations, comparisons, or switch cases

**Where to place:**
- Types always go in `lib/types.ts`
- Runtime constants go in `lib/constants.ts`, importing their type from `lib/types.ts`
- Co-locate in the component file only if the set is used exclusively there
